Level 2 - Task 4 — Caching
-----------------------------------------------------------

Cache saves dependencies between runs — avoids re-downloading every time.

How it works:
- Run 1 → Cache Miss → pip install → cache saved
- Run 2 → Cache Hit → Using cached → fast!

Key components:
  uses: actions/cache@v3
  with:
    path: ~/.cache/pip
    key: ${{ runner.os }}-pip-${{ hashFiles('requirements.txt') }}

path → where cache is stored
key  → unique cache identifier

hashFiles('requirements.txt'):
- Reads requirements.txt content → generates unique hash
- requirements.txt changed → new hash → Cache Miss → fresh install
- requirements.txt same → same hash → Cache Hit → skip install!

Cache order (important!):
1. Checkout
2. Setup Python
3. Cache ← must be before install!
4. Install dependencies
5. Run code

Real world benefit:
- Small project (3 packages) → negligible difference
- Large project (100+ packages) → 5min → 30sec!

Note: Runner variance (2-3s difference) is normal — shared machines!





-----------------------------------------------------------
Level 2 - Task 5 — Artifacts
-----------------------------------------------------------

Artifacts save build outputs (files, reports) for later use.

How it works:
- Job runs → file generated → upload as artifact
- Team downloads from GitHub UI OR next job uses it

Upload artifact:
  uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
  with:
    name: test-report
    path: test-report.txt

Download artifact (in next job):
  uses: actions/download-artifact@v4
  with:
    name: test-report

Access artifacts:
1. GitHub UI → Actions → Run → Artifacts section → Download
2. GitHub API → programmatically download
3. Next job → download-artifact action (most common!)

Caching vs Artifacts:
- Cache   → speed up installs (pip packages, node_modules)
- Artifacts → save outputs (reports, binaries, ML models)

Real world use cases:
- Test reports → QA team downloads and reviews
- Compiled binaries → deploy to server
- ML models → train in Job 1, deploy in Job 2
- Coverage reports → track code quality

Note: Only repo access members can download artifacts from GitHub UI!








-----------------------------------------------------------
Level 3 - Task 1 — Multi-Job Workflows (needs:)
-----------------------------------------------------------

Run jobs in sequence using needs: keyword.

Structure:
  build → test → deploy

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps: ...

  test:
    needs: build        # waits for build to complete
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps: ...

  deploy:
    needs: test         # waits for test to complete
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps: ...

Key behaviors:
- build fails → test skipped → deploy skipped
- Each job runs on fresh runner
- needs: takes single job or list: [build, test]

Real world pipeline:
build → test → security-scan → deploy-staging → deploy-prod